AH = "This lesson summarises ideas from Atomic Habits by James Clear in our own words."
L = {}
L['ah-tiny-gains'] = dict(
intro="When we want to change our lives, we often look for one big move: a strict new diet, a dramatic new routine, a resolution that will fix everything at once. Atomic Habits begins from the opposite direction. Its central argument is that small, almost unnoticeable improvements, repeated consistently, can add up to large changes over time. " + AH,
sections=[
("Why small feels pointless at first", "A tiny improvement rarely produces a visible result on the day you make it. Reading five pages does not make you wise by evening. One healthy meal does not change your body by morning. Because the reward is not immediate, small actions are easy to dismiss as not worth the effort. The book points out that this is exactly why many people give up on good habits too early: they expect quick results from changes that only show their value over time."),
("The idea of compounding", "The book borrows the idea of compounding, familiar from savings, to describe habits. When a small gain builds on yesterday's small gain, the effect grows over time instead of simply adding up. The same works in reverse. Small negative choices, such as skipping sleep or putting off one task, can also compound quietly. The message is not that every moment is critical, but that the direction you are heading in matters a great deal more than any single day."),
("The plateau before the breakthrough", "The book describes a frustrating stage where you are putting in effort but seeing little change. It compares progress to ice warming slowly in a cold room: nothing seems to happen until, at a certain point, it starts to melt. Earlier effort was not wasted; it was building up. Understanding this stage can help you keep going when results seem invisible, instead of concluding that the habit 'does not work'."),
("Trajectory over results", "Because small habits take time to show results, the book suggests paying attention to your trajectory rather than your current position. Someone with modest savings who saves a little each month may be on a better path than someone with more money who spends it all. Someone who is unfit but walking every day may be heading somewhere better than someone fitter who has stopped moving. Asking 'Which way am I heading?' is often more useful than 'Where am I now?'"),
("What this means for you", "The practical lesson is to make your next improvement smaller than you think it needs to be, and then repeat it. Small changes are easier to start, easier to keep, and less likely to collapse on a busy day. Over weeks and months, they can reshape routines in a way that dramatic bursts of effort often cannot."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: Priya's five pages", "Priya, a bank employee in Ahmedabad, wanted to read more but kept buying books she never finished. She decided to read just five pages each night after dinner, no more required. Some nights she read more, some nights only the five. For the first few weeks it felt like almost nothing. By the end of a few months she realised she had finished several books, more than in the previous year. The change came from consistency, not effort. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Small improvements can add up to large changes when repeated over time.","Results from good habits are often delayed, which is why people quit too early.","Progress can feel invisible before it becomes visible; keep going through the plateau.","Watch your direction of travel more than your current position."],
reflect=["Which habit did you give up on because it did not show results quickly enough?","What direction is one area of your life heading in, if nothing changes?"],
)
L['ah-systems'] = dict(
intro="Most advice about change begins with goals: lose weight, save money, get a promotion. Atomic Habits argues that goals are useful for setting a direction, but that the real work happens in systems, the daily processes that lead to results. This lesson explores the difference and why it matters. " + AH,
sections=[
("Goals versus systems", "A goal is the result you want. A system is the set of habits and routines that produce results. If your goal is a clean room, your system might be putting things away each evening. If your goal is to pass an exam, your system might be a daily study block. The book's point is that people with the same goal can get very different results because their systems differ. The goal alone does not create the change; the system does."),
("Why goals alone can fall short", "The book raises a few problems with relying on goals alone. Winners and losers often share the same goals, so the goal cannot be what separates them. Reaching a goal is only a moment; if the system that got you there stops, the result can fade. Goals can also make happiness feel postponed: 'I will be content once I reach it'. And once a goal is achieved, motivation can disappear, leaving people to slip back to old patterns."),
("Falling in love with the process", "A systems approach shifts attention from the finish line to the daily practice. Instead of measuring yourself only by whether you reached a target, you ask whether you showed up for the routine today. This tends to be more satisfying, because you can succeed every day you follow the process, rather than only on the day you reach a goal. It also encourages long-term thinking: the system can keep running after any single goal is met."),
("Designing a simple system", "A simple system usually answers three questions: what will I do, when will I do it, and where? For example: 'I will walk for twenty minutes after lunch, around the office building.' The system should be small enough to survive busy days. It can be adjusted over time as you learn what works. The goal stays in the background as a compass, while the system does the daily work."),
("Systems in money and work", "This idea applies well beyond fitness. At work, a system might be clearing your inbox at set times instead of reacting all day. With money, it might be noting expenses every Sunday, or moving a fixed amount to savings on payday so you do not have to decide each month. These examples are illustrative only, and money decisions should be based on your own situation. This is not financial advice."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the exam system", "Rahul, a commerce student in Jaipur, set a goal of scoring well in his accountancy exam. In past years he had studied in panicked bursts just before tests. This time he set a system: one hour of practice problems every evening at his desk, starting right after dinner. Some evenings went badly, but the routine held. By exam week he felt calmer, not because the goal had changed but because the system had been doing the work quietly for months. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Goals set direction; systems are the daily processes that create results.","Goals alone can postpone happiness and fade once reached.","Focusing on the process lets you succeed every day you show up.","A good system says what, when and where, and is small enough for busy days."],
reflect=["Think of a goal you have. What daily system would move you towards it?","Have you ever reached a goal and then slipped back? What system was missing?"],
)
L['ah-identity'] = dict(
intro="Why do some habits stick while others fade? Atomic Habits suggests that the deepest layer of change is not what you do or what you achieve, but who you believe you are. This lesson explores the idea of identity-based habits: building habits around the kind of person you want to become. " + AH,
sections=[
("Three layers of change", "The book describes change happening at three levels. The outer layer is outcomes, such as losing weight or writing a book. The middle layer is processes, the habits and routines you follow. The inner layer is identity, your beliefs about yourself. Many people start with outcomes: 'I want to run a marathon.' The book suggests starting from identity instead: 'I am a person who moves my body every day.'"),
("Why identity makes habits stick", "When a habit is tied to identity, it becomes less about willpower and more about being consistent with yourself. Compare two people offered a cigarette. One says, 'No thanks, I'm trying to quit.' The other says, 'No thanks, I don't smoke.' The first still sees themselves as a smoker resisting temptation. The second has shifted identity. The book uses examples like this to show how the story we tell about ourselves shapes our choices."),
("Every action is a vote", "A helpful idea from the book is that each action is like a vote for the kind of person you want to be. Reading one page is a vote for being a reader. Tidying your desk is a vote for being an organised person. You do not need a unanimous vote; you just need more votes for the identity you want than against it. This takes the pressure off any single day: a missed workout is one vote, not a verdict."),
("Choosing your identity", "The book suggests a two-step process: decide the type of person you want to be, then prove it to yourself with small wins. Asking 'What would a healthy person do here?' or 'What would a calm parent do?' can guide everyday decisions. It is worth choosing identities that reflect your own values rather than other people's expectations, and holding them flexibly, because people grow and change."),
("A note of care", "Identity can also work against us. Beliefs such as 'I'm bad with money' or 'I'm not a morning person' can become self-fulfilling. Noticing these labels, and asking whether they are facts or habits of thought, is an important part of change. Be gentle here: shifting how you see yourself takes time."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: 'I'm someone who cooks'", "Ananya, who lived in a hostel and then a shared flat in Bengaluru, had always told friends, 'I can't cook.' After moving out, she wanted to eat better. Instead of setting a goal like 'cook five days a week', she started thinking of herself as 'someone who can make simple meals'. Each time she made poha or dal, she treated it as a small vote for that identity. Months later, cooking felt like part of who she was, not a chore she forced herself to do. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Change happens at the level of outcomes, processes and identity.","Habits tied to identity tend to last longer than habits tied to goals.","Each small action is a vote for the person you want to become.","Notice limiting labels about yourself and question whether they are true."],
reflect=["Which label about yourself might be holding you back?","What small action today would be a vote for the person you want to be?"],
)
L['ah-four-laws'] = dict(
intro="Atomic Habits offers a simple framework for building good habits and breaking bad ones, which the author calls the Four Laws of Behaviour Change. These laws are based on a model of how habits form: a cue, a craving, a response and a reward. This lesson walks through each in plain language. " + AH,
sections=[
("The habit loop", "The book describes habits as a loop. A cue is something that triggers the behaviour, such as seeing your phone. A craving is the desire for a change in state, such as wanting distraction. The response is the action, such as unlocking the phone. The reward is what you get, such as a moment of entertainment. Over time, the loop runs so quickly that it feels automatic. The four laws map onto these four steps."),
("Make it obvious", "The first law relates to the cue. If you want a habit to happen, make the cue easy to see. Put your running shoes by the door. Keep a book on your pillow. Write a specific plan, such as 'I will meditate for two minutes at 7 a.m. in the bedroom'. The book suggests that many people do not lack motivation so much as clarity about when and where they will act."),
("Make it attractive", "The second law relates to craving. Habits that feel appealing are more likely to happen. One approach the book describes is pairing something you need to do with something you enjoy, for example listening to a favourite podcast only while walking. Another is spending time with people for whom the habit is normal, so it feels like part of fitting in."),
("Make it easy", "The third law relates to the response. The less effort a habit needs, the more likely it is to happen. Reducing steps, such as preparing your gym bag the night before, helps. The book also introduces the Two-Minute Rule: scale a new habit down so it takes about two minutes to start. 'Read before bed' becomes 'read one page'. Once starting is easy, continuing often follows."),
("Make it satisfying", "The fourth law relates to reward. We repeat what feels good. Because many good habits pay off only later, the book suggests adding a small immediate satisfaction, such as ticking off a habit tracker. For breaking bad habits, the laws are inverted: make the cue invisible, the craving unattractive, the action difficult and the result unsatisfying."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: Vikram's water habit", "Vikram, who works long shifts at a call centre in Gurugram, often went hours without drinking water. Using the four laws, he placed a filled bottle on his desk before each shift (obvious), chose a bottle he liked (attractive), refilled it at a cooler right next to his bay (easy), and marked a tally on a sticky note each time he finished it (satisfying). None of these steps was dramatic, but together they made the habit much more likely. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Habits follow a loop: cue, craving, response, reward.","Make good habits obvious, attractive, easy and satisfying.","The Two-Minute Rule shrinks a new habit so it is easy to start.","To break a habit, invert the laws: invisible, unattractive, difficult, unsatisfying."],
reflect=["Pick one habit you want. Which of the four laws is weakest for it right now?","What is a two-minute version of a habit you have been putting off?"],
)
L['ah-stacking'] = dict(
intro="One of the most practical ideas in Atomic Habits is habit stacking: linking a new habit to one you already do every day. Because your existing routines already happen reliably, they can act as natural reminders for something new. This lesson explains how stacking works and how to use it well. " + AH,
sections=[
("Using what already happens", "Every day contains dozens of habits you no longer think about: brushing your teeth, making tea, locking the door, sitting down at your desk. These actions happen at fairly predictable times and places. Habit stacking treats each one as a hook. Instead of trying to remember a new habit out of nowhere, you attach it to an old one. The old habit becomes the cue for the new one."),
("The stacking formula", "The book offers a simple sentence pattern: 'After I [current habit], I will [new habit].' For example: 'After I pour my morning tea, I will write one line in my journal.' Or: 'After I sit down for lunch, I will put my phone face down.' Writing the sentence makes the plan concrete. It answers the question of when the habit will happen, which is often what goes missing in vague intentions."),
("Choosing the right anchor", "Not every existing habit makes a good anchor. A good anchor happens at the same frequency you want for the new habit, at a suitable place, and at a moment when you have the time and energy. If you want to stretch daily, attaching it to something you do only on weekends will not work. If you want to read, attaching it to a moment when children are demanding attention may not either. A little thought about timing makes stacking far more reliable."),
("Building chains", "Once one stack is working, you can link more habits onto it, creating a small routine. For instance: after I pour my tea, I will write one line in my journal; after I write my line, I will look at my plan for the day. These chains can turn scattered intentions into a calm, predictable start or end to the day. It is wise to add links slowly, one at a time, so the chain does not become overwhelming."),
("When stacking does not stick", "If a stack keeps failing, the usual reasons are that the anchor is unreliable, the new habit is too big, or the timing is wrong. Rather than blaming yourself, adjust the design: choose a different anchor, shrink the habit, or move it to a calmer moment."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the pressure cooker stretch", "Sunita, a homemaker in Lucknow, wanted to stretch every day but kept forgetting. She noticed that every morning she waited a few minutes for the pressure cooker to whistle. She wrote a stack: 'After I put the cooker on the stove, I will do three gentle stretches beside the counter.' The waiting time had always been there; now it had a purpose. Within a few weeks, the stretches felt as automatic as the cooker itself. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Habit stacking links a new habit to an existing daily habit.","Use the formula: 'After I [current habit], I will [new habit].'","Choose anchors with the right timing, place and frequency.","Add links to a chain slowly; adjust the design if a stack fails."],
reflect=["List three things you do every single day. Which could anchor a new habit?","Which daily waiting moment could hold a small, useful action?"],
)
L['ah-environment'] = dict(
intro="We often assume that people with good habits simply have more willpower. Atomic Habits suggests a different view: people with good habits often have environments that make those habits easier. This lesson explores environment design and the Two-Minute Rule, two tools for making good choices the default. " + AH,
sections=[
("Environment shapes behaviour", "The book argues that much of our behaviour is a response to our surroundings. If biscuits are on the counter, we eat biscuits. If the TV remote is on the sofa, we watch TV. Small details of where things are placed quietly guide what we do, often without us noticing. This means that changing the environment can be a more reliable way to change behaviour than trying harder."),
("Designing for good habits", "To make a good habit easier, make its cues visible and its tools close at hand. Leave a fruit bowl where you can see it. Keep a water bottle on your desk. Put a guitar on a stand in the living room instead of in a case in a cupboard. The book also suggests creating clear associations between places and activities: one chair for reading, one table for work. When a place is linked to one activity, being there gently prompts it."),
("The Two-Minute Rule", "The Two-Minute Rule says that when you start a new habit, it should take less than two minutes to do. 'Exercise for thirty minutes' becomes 'put on your walking shoes'. 'Study for the exam' becomes 'open your notes'. The idea is to master the habit of showing up before trying to improve it. A habit must be established before it can be improved, and a tiny version is much easier to establish."),
("Why starting small works", "The opening of an activity is often the hardest part. Once your shoes are on, walking for a while feels natural. Once the notebook is open, writing a few lines is easier. The Two-Minute Rule lowers the barrier to starting so that consistency can grow. It is fine to stop after two minutes; the point is to make showing up automatic. Over time, the habit can expand naturally."),
("Combining both tools", "Environment design and the Two-Minute Rule work well together. Arrange your space so the first two minutes of a good habit are effortless, and so the first steps of a bad habit are less convenient. This combination reduces how often you need to rely on willpower at all."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the yoga mat by the bed", "Deepak, a teacher in Bhopal, wanted to do morning yoga but rarely managed it. His mat was rolled up behind a cupboard. He moved it to lie open beside his bed every night, and set a two-minute rule: on waking, he only had to sit on the mat and take a few slow breaths. Most mornings, once he was on the mat, he continued for a while longer. Some mornings he stopped at two minutes, and that was fine too. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Surroundings quietly guide behaviour, often more than willpower.","Make cues for good habits visible and tools easy to reach.","The Two-Minute Rule shrinks a new habit so it is easy to start.","Master showing up first; improve the habit later."],
reflect=["What in your surroundings makes an unwanted habit easy?","What is the two-minute version of a habit you want to build?"],
)
